A||||5||2FAST4U||How to remove tinting on defogger rear window||||1||
Z||000000||2FAST4U||01-12-2000||09:50 PM||||I have a 85 GT that has a purpleish window tint on it. I'd like to remove the tinting but I'm not sure how to do this without damaging the defogger wires.<BR>Anybody got any suggestions.<P>Thanks<P>Joe<BR>2fast4u||||reg||1
Z||000001||Boxdin||01-12-2000||10:04 PM||||I just did this very job. I probably ruined the function of the defrost, but the appearence of the defrost is fine. I used soapy water and a razpr blade scrape tool and went at it. Let it soak ||||reg||6
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-12-2000||10:57 PM||||There was/is a rear window with the defogger on eBay.  If you mess up the grid just a little bit, you can fix it fairly easily.  I don't know what you'd do if your screwed it up royally, though.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Luperman||01-12-2000||11:19 PM||||if you use a vinegar and water solution it will pretty much eat the gooy glue off the window after you peel the initial film off then just windex it and wipe ,  if you damage some of the lines they make a repair thats in a small bottle that you brush over the damaged area . ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||01-13-2000||12:23 AM||||I have heard that you soak the window then cover it with a plastic trash bag and let it sit for a while. Out of the sun. Then it will pull off and you will need to clean the glue||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||01-13-2000||12:35 AM||||A strong solution of ammonia is very good at removing the glue. Probably take a razor blade and cut some lines into the tint for the ammonia to soak into the glue through will help. Just dont cut across the defroster grid lines. I have not heard Tom's suggestion before, but I will try it next time. After the tint is pulled off, soak the glue and use an old credit card,driver's license or bondo spreader to scrape the loose glue off. Auto body supply stores and detail supply shops have a product specially made for tint removal, but the ammonia works about as well for a lot less $$$$$||||reg||1
